    "text": "To determine the number of diagonals in the fifth polygon of the sequence, we first need to identify the type of each polygon and then use the formula for the number of diagonals in a polygon. The formula for the number of diagonals in a polygon with \\( n \\) sides is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-3)}{2}\n\\]\n\nWe are given the first four polygons and their number of diagonals:\n\n1. A triangle (3 sides) has 0 diagonals.\n2. A quadrilateral (4 sides) has 2 diagonals.\n3. A pentagon (5 sides) has 5 diagonals.\n4. A hexagon (6 sides) has 9 diagonals.\n\nWe need to find the fifth polygon and its number of diagonals. Let's identify the fifth polygon by examining the sequence of polygons provided in the problem. The sequence of polygons is increasing in the number of sides, and the number of sides can be deduced from the number of vertices in the polygon.\n\n- The first polygon has 3 sides (a triangle).\n- The second polygon has 4 sides (a quadrilateral).\n- The third polygon has 5 sides (a pentagon).\n- The fourth polygon has 6 sides (a hexagon).\n\nThe fifth polygon in the sequence will have 7 sides (a heptagon). Now, we can use the formula to find the number of diagonals in a heptagon (7 sides):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7(7-3)}{2} = \\frac{7 \\cdot 4}{2} = \\frac{28}{2} = 14\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of diagonals in the fifth polygon is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).",
